WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.020
<v Maya>Welcome to the Chief of Staff Playbook, the show where we help chiefs of staff get better at the job, one episode at a time.</v>

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:15.300
<v Maya>I'm Maya, and I'm here with Marcus, who has worked alongside executives across tech, healthcare, and finance as a chief of staff and advisor.</v>

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.560
<v Maya>Marcus, thanks for being here.</v>

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.140
<v Marcus>Thanks for having me, Maya.</v>

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.300
<v Marcus>Great topic to kick off with.</v>

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.860
<v Maya>So today we're talking to first time chiefs of staff, or anyone who remembers being one.</v>

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.700
<v Maya>You've got three takeaways you wish someone had handed you on day one.</v>

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.740
<v Maya>But first, a quick level set, because the title means different things at different companies.</v>

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.480
<v Maya>What does a chief of staff actually do.</v>

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.040
<v Marcus>Great question, because the role is famously fuzzy.</v>

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.840
<v Marcus>At its core, a chief of staff is a force multiplier for a senior leader, usually the C E O.</v>

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:55.720
<v Marcus>You take the things that need the leader's judgment but not necessarily the leader's time, and you make them happen.</v>

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:01.740
<v Marcus>If the C E O is the lead singer, you're the producer and the tour manager rolled into one.</v>

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:08.140
<v Marcus>You run the operating system around them so they can spend their time where only they can add value.</v>

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.380
<v Maya>Producer and tour manager.</v>

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.820
<v Maya>Okay, takeaway number one.</v>

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:13.960
<v Maya>What is it.</v>

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.280
<v Marcus>Multiply the principal, don't try to be the principal.</v>

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.700
<v Maya>That sounds obvious, but I bet it's where most new chiefs of staff go wrong.</v>

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.540
<v Marcus>Exactly.</v>

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.960
<v Marcus>You get hired because you're capable and driven and full of opinions.</v>

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.240
<v Marcus>Then you start acting like a second C E O, making calls that aren't yours to make.</v>

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.240
<v Marcus>Or you swing the other way and become a glorified assistant, just scheduling and taking notes.</v>

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.080
<v Marcus>Both miss the point.</v>

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:45.960
<v Marcus>Your job is to extend your principal's reach and judgment, not replace either one.</v>

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.180
<v Maya>How do you know where that line is.</v>

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.320
<v Marcus>Three moves.</v>

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.920
<v Marcus>First, learn how your principal thinks.</v>

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:56.540
<v Marcus>In your first thirty days, study them like it's your job, because it is.</v>

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.160
<v Marcus>How do they make decisions.</v>

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.160
<v Marcus>What do they care about most.</v>

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:01.660
<v Marcus>What annoys them.</v>

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.080
<v Marcus>What does a good week look like for them.</v>

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:09.240
<v Marcus>The better you can predict their reaction, the more you can act on their behalf with confidence.</v>

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:12.520
<v Maya>So you're building a mental model of the person.</v>

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:13.400
<v Marcus>Exactly.</v>

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.960
<v Marcus>Second, get ruthless about their time and attention.</v>

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.400
<v Marcus>Audit the calendar.</v>

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.780
<v Marcus>Which meetings actually need them.</v>

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:23.280
<v Marcus>Which decisions actually need them.</v>

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.900
<v Marcus>Your superpower is giving them back hours and focus.</v>

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.860
<v Marcus>Every recurring meeting should earn its place.</v>

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.880
<v Marcus>If it doesn't need the C E O, it doesn't get the C E O.</v>

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.220
<v Maya>I imagine that ruffles feathers, telling people they can't have time with the boss.</v>

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.240
<v Marcus>It does, which is why the third move matters.</v>

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.140
<v Marcus>Communicate the why.</v>

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:49.620
<v Marcus>You're not gatekeeping, you're protecting the company's most scarce resource, which is the leader's attention.</v>

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.260
<v Marcus>When people understand that, most of them get on board.</v>

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.560
<v Marcus>And the ones who don't, well, that's useful information too.</v>

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.160
<v Maya>Okay, so takeaway one is multiply the principal, don't try to be the principal.</v>

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.220
<v Maya>Learn how they think, protect their time, and explain the why.</v>

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:09.820
<v Maya>What's number two.</v>

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.840
<v Marcus>Run the operating system.</v>

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.040
<v Maya>The meetings, the follow through, the unglamorous machinery.</v>

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.120
<v Marcus>The unglamorous machinery is the job.</v>

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.200
<v Marcus>Most executives don't fail for lack of vision.</v>

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:29.580
<v Marcus>They fail because decisions don't get made, or get made twice, or get made and then nobody follows through.</v>

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.940
<v Marcus>As chief of staff, you own the machine.</v>

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.720
<v Marcus>The weekly leadership meeting, the decision log, the follow up.</v>

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.520
<v Maya>Give me the concrete version.</v>

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.180
<v Maya>What does a good operating system look like.</v>

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:43.080
<v Marcus>Three parts.</v>

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:47.260
<v Marcus>First, a real weekly leadership meeting with a tight agenda.</v>

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.340
<v Marcus>What matters this week, what decisions do we need, what's stuck.</v>

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:52.600
<v Marcus>You run it.</v>

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:58.580
<v Marcus>You keep it on time, you keep it honest, and you make sure it ends with clear owners and deadlines.</v>

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:05.220
<v Marcus>If your staff meeting is a status parade where everyone reads slides, kill that format and rebuild it.</v>

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:06.860
<v Maya>Status parade.</v>

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:08.920
<v Maya>I've sat through a few of those.</v>

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.160
<v Marcus>Everyone has.</v>

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:12.320
<v Marcus>Second, write decisions down.</v>

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.040
<v Marcus>A simple decision log.</v>

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.360
<v Marcus>What was decided, who decided it, when, and why.</v>

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.940
<v Marcus>You would not believe how much executive time gets wasted re litigating decisions nobody wrote down.</v>

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:26.900
<v Marcus>The log ends that.</v>

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.280
<v Maya>That seems so small, but I can see how it compounds.</v>

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:31.600
<v Marcus>It really does.</v>

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.340
<v Marcus>Third, be the follow through machine.</v>

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:39.540
<v Marcus>After every meeting, the notes go out within hours, with owners and dates.</v>

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:41.140
<v Marcus>Then you check.</v>

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:44.060
<v Marcus>Not in a nagging way, in a steady way.</v>

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:45.800
<v Marcus>Did the thing happen.</v>

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:47.900
<v Marcus>If not, what's in the way.</v>

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.440
<v Marcus>Accountability without drama.</v>

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:53.180
<v Marcus>That's the chief of staff's signature move.</v>

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:57.180
<v Maya>So takeaway two is run the operating system.</v>

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:01.060
<v Maya>Tight meetings, written decisions, relentless follow through.</v>

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:02.680
<v Maya>What's the third.</v>

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.760
<v Marcus>Build trust through discretion and candor.</v>

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:07.740
<v Maya>Those two sound like opposites.</v>

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:12.080
<v Maya>Discretion is holding things close, candor is saying the hard thing.</v>

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:17.700
<v Marcus>They're two sides of the same coin, and the combination is what makes a chief of staff invaluable.</v>

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:19.640
<v Marcus>Discretion first.</v>

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.580
<v Marcus>You will hear everything.</v>

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:27.520
<v Marcus>Board politics, compensation, who's struggling, what's really going on.</v>

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:32.360
<v Marcus>If you leak, even once, even something small, you're done.</v>

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.360
<v Marcus>People need to know that what they tell you stays with you.</v>

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.000
<v Marcus>That trust is the currency the whole role runs on.</v>

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:41.140
<v Maya>And candor.</v>

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:43.040
<v Marcus>Candor is the other half.</v>

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:47.120
<v Marcus>The higher someone rises, the less honest feedback they get.</v>

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:48.700
<v Marcus>Everyone filters.</v>

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.860
<v Marcus>Your job is to be the person who doesn't filter.</v>

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:59.780
<v Marcus>When your principal's big idea has a hole in it, you say so, privately and respectfully, before it goes public.</v>

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:05.860
<v Marcus>When they're about to walk into a meeting unprepared for the real dynamic, you brief them on it.</v>

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:09.040
<v Maya>That takes guts, telling your boss they're wrong.</v>

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:14.760
<v Marcus>It does, and that's why most people won't do it, and why it's so valuable when you do.</v>

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:18.340
<v Marcus>The key is private candor, public loyalty.</v>

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.340
<v Marcus>Challenge in the room with just the two of you.</v>

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:23.880
<v Marcus>Outside that room, you're aligned.</v>

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:29.340
<v Marcus>Never surprise your principal, and never let anyone see daylight between you in public.</v>

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:31.880
<v Maya>Private candor, public loyalty.</v>

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:33.600
<v Maya>I love that framing.</v>

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:36.820
<v Maya>Let me recap the three, and you tell me if I've got them right.</v>

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:41.220
<v Maya>One, multiply the principal, don't try to be the principal.</v>

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:43.980
<v Maya>Two, run the operating system.</v>

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.420
<v Maya>Three, build trust through discretion and candor.</v>

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:48.760
<v Marcus>You nailed it.</v>

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:52.020
<v Maya>If someone takes one thing from today, what should it be.</v>

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:55.760
<v Marcus>The best chiefs of staff are egoless force multipliers.</v>

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.420
<v Marcus>Your wins are invisible by design.</v>

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:05.080
<v Marcus>When the leader shines and the organization hums, that's your work, even if nobody claps for it.</v>

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.180
<v Marcus>If you need the credit, this is the wrong job.</v>

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.460
<v Marcus>If you love the impact, there's nothing better.</v>

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:13.700
<v Maya>Egoless force multiplier.</v>

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:16.320
<v Maya>Marcus, this was fantastic.</v>

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:17.980
<v Maya>What's coming tomorrow.</v>

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.540
<v Marcus>Tomorrow we go deeper on the relationship with your principal.</v>

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:29.100
<v Marcus>How to run a great weekly one on one with your C E O, and the five questions that surface problems before they become crises.</v>

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:30.240
<v Maya>Can't wait.</v>

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.940
<v Maya>Thanks for listening to the Chief of Staff Playbook.</v>

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:36.520
<v Maya>If this helped you, share it with one person stepping into the role.</v>

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:37.720
<v Maya>We'll see you tomorrow.</v>

